paragraphs.en:
Xiangru wished to tempt Zhuo Wenjun;
how much feeling he had, already ten parts in ten.
Over the wall the rouged peach and plum are lovely;
in the wind, where will they fall in scattered profusion?

Below the curved balustrade lies the lotus pond,
and by the pond, among the flowers, two people talk.
Fragrant mist drifts thick, the spring is warm and mellow;
I make new words and sing "White Ramie."
The moon turns the flower shade into a woolen rug;
together we pull down long boughs and red petals fall like rain.
The wind stirs pure fragrance, the fragrance fills our robes;
Miss Jia first treads the dance in the spring sun.
Her gauze sleeves lightly brush the begonia boughs
and startle the parrots sleeping among the flowers.

I have strayed into Peach Spring, the flowers in riot;
how much feeling I have and cannot put into words.
Twin green coils pinned low with gold ▩,
trim spring blouses cut from green ramie.
The east wind has just opened the paired-stem flowers;
do not let the thick boughs fight the wind and rain.
Fluttering immortal sleeves, shadows swaying,
among the cassia shade Chang'e dances.
Fine affairs are not done before sorrow must follow;
do not make new words to teach the parrots.

Secluded bamboo rustles as if it had a voice;
ancient trees bend as if they had feeling.
Wild roots coil and twist, trailing moss;
old trunks arch and curl, rivaling wind and thunder.
In the breast there is naturally a cave of creation;
its wonders, how could one tell them to bystanders?
Wei Yan and Wen Yuke have already become ghosts;
how many are there who know the leaked secrets of Heaven?
By the clear window, in silence, I face it calmly,
loving to watch the divine samadhi of the magic ink.

The lotus-patterned curtain is warm, the incense like a thread;
outside the window fine red apricot rain falls.
From the top of the tower, a remnant dream and the fifth-watch bell;
a mockingbird cries in the magnolia hollow.
The swallows' day grows long, the inner chambers deep;
lazy and wordless, she stops her golden needle.
Beneath the flowers pairs of butterflies fly,
vying to chase fallen petals into the courtyard shade.
A tender chill lightly passes through her green gauze skirt;
in vain she faces the spring wind, secretly heartbroken.
Who can guess this tender feeling?
Among the hundred flowers mandarin ducks dance.
Spring color lies deep in the Huang family's four gardens,
deep red and pale green reflected on the window gauze.
In the courtyard fragrant grass, the spring heart is bitter;
lightly she lifts the bead curtain to watch the falling flowers.

The wheat has just conceived, young swallows slant;
in the south garden pomegranate flowers bloom everywhere.
At the green window the seamstress, scissors in hand,
means to try cutting a crimson skirt from purple haze.
In the plum-rain season the rain curtain is fine;
orioles warble in the locust shade, swallows enter the curtain.
Another year's scenery has grown old;
chinaberry blossoms fall and bamboo shoots show their sharp tips.
By hand she plucks a green apricot to strike the oriole;
wind passes the south pavilion, the sun's shadow lingers.
The lotus leaves are already fragrant, the pond water full;
in the depths of the blue waves cormorants bathe.
On the rattan couch and bamboo mat, ripples of pattern;
the screen's picture is Xiao and Xiang, a smear of cloud.
Languid, she cannot bear to wake from her noon dream;
half the window is slanting sun, about to turn to western dusk.

The autumn wind sighs, the autumn dew congeals;
the autumn moon is radiant, the autumn water green.
One cry, two cries, the wild geese return;
again I hear leaves of the parasol tree by the golden well.
Under the bed a hundred insects chirp;
on the bed the fair one's pearl tears drip.
Her husband is ten thousand li away at war;
tonight the moon is white over Jade Gate Pass.
She would cut a new robe but the scissors are cold;
softly she calls her maid to bring the iron.
The fire in the iron has died and she never noticed;
she plucks the Qin zither gently and scratches her head.
In the small pond the lotus is spent, the plantain yellow;
on the mandarin-duck tiles new frost clings.
Old sorrow and new regret cannot be borne,
and still she hears crickets chirp in the inner room.

A branch of plum shadow lies across the window;
the wind is sharp in the west corridor, the moonlight bright.
The brazier fire is not out; she stirs it with golden tongs
and soon calls the girl in double buns to change the tea kettle.
The forest leaves keep startling at the half-night frost;
a turning wind drifts snow into the long corridor.
For no reason, one night, a dream of longing
lies wholly in the icy river, on the ancient battlefield.
The window is full of red sun, like the warmth of spring;
sorrow locks her brows and sleep-marks show.
In the vase the small plum, its cheek half open;
shy and wordless, she embroiders a pair of mandarin ducks.
Sharp and cutting, the frost wind sweeps the northern forest;
a cold bird cries at the moon, and it plucks the heart.
Before the lamp, tears for the one she thinks of
drip onto the small needle threaded with silk.

A broken mirror made whole again: there will be a time;
the magpies of the Heavenly Ford help the happy meeting.
From now on the Old Man of the Moon ties the cord;
do not resent the east wind in front of the cuckoo.

Spears and shields filled the eye, clashing everywhere;
jade shattered, flowers flew, the mandarin duck lost her mate.
Her scattered remains lay in disorder; who buried them?
A blood-stained wandering soul, with no one to speak to.
Gaotang once came down: the maiden of Wushan;
the broken mirror parted again, my heart bitter with grief.
From this parting we are both lost in the vast and dim;
between Heaven and the human world, news is blocked.
